  it together in a nice little bow however  we did also make it very prominent in  the third verse as i say the lines  [Music]  it kind of comes in louder as if you  have just opened a window  whoa  i think it's really small details like  that that  kind of help build towards the the  narrative and the theme that that i was  talking about earlier okay so after the  ambience was recording my guitar so i  recorded my guitar on my pc with my blue  yeti microphone  and uh the software of reaper i did  attempt to play with a metronome that  was at uh 92 beats per minute bpm  but i still find playing to a metronome  pretty difficult so it took me a good  few tries to get it right the best way  that i found to do this was to focus on  trying to get at least three decent  takes of each part of the song so i kind  of  broke up the song  so i did the verses individually the  choruses individually  this alleviated the pressure on myself  because i wasn't trying to play the  whole song through  perfectly my concentration uh kept  breaking my timing kept going strange if  i had tried to play the whole entire  song through so at the end of the day it  worked better for me for jamie for the  song as a whole for me to play it bit by  bit by recording multiple takes of each  section jaime could then  use them and hopefully there would be at  least one good one in the bunch which  there were so that's good we did have  some audio issues where because i am not  in his lovely soundproof studio  you could hear some background noise  unfortunately i did try to  soundproof my room as best that i could  i do have some soundproof panels on my  wall  i tried to get a sheet next to the  microphone  but ultimately it's not going to be as  good as the studio  with my green screen most notably the  background noise was actually from the  metronome coming from my headphones so i  had to turn that right down which made  playing to a  metronome even harder  um but we did it in the end and jamie  did  amazing amazing job of removing that  background noise so thank you jimmy  here's a little insight into jamie's  workflow when cleaning up the guitar the  first thing i'm using on everything is  noise reduction because it's recording  in her room  there was computer noise ambient noise  hiss from the preamps over many tracks  playing together this noise will add up  so just doing a bit of reduction on each  track can just lower that noise a little  bit i just play a before and after on  the guitar  it's probably quite a subtle thing on  the guitar next  i'm doing a little bit of dynamic eq  there was a bit of a node here that was  jumping up and down  and as you see it's around here it's  just kind of tucking it away as it comes  in then an eq  there's an eq i use a lot shep 73 it's a  1073 emulation and this i'm just using  to make the track sound a little bit  better okay so vocals  this is the fun part  so once the ambience and the guitar was  all put together it was time for me to  record my vocals i did this again on my  pc using my blue yeti microphone and the  software of reaper  on my microphones on an arm so i could  make it stand up so i could stand up and  sing into it which is great very much  like the guitar parts i did  sing each part individually versus  individually courses individually and  the end as well because i always feel  like the end is something you really  want to get right and then i sense jamie  multiple takes so we could kind of mix  and match to see what we liked best i  can be really picky when it comes to my  own voice so there were a good few weeks  where i was re-singing parts  however  at the end of the day i just had to know  when to stop you can keep working on  something forever and it's never going  to be perfect  so for me what really helps with this is  to give myself soft deadlines and if i  know that i'm coming upon one i then  need to reflect and ask myself  you know  is this project actually  bad do i need to spend mo